## Data Flow & Best Practices

### Asynchronous Aggregation

- Use `Promise.all` or `Promise.allSettled` to run subtasks in parallel.
- Example:

    ```ts
    const [exaData, crunchbaseData] = await Promise.allSettled([
      exa.searchAndContents(domain, { text: true }),
      fetchCrunchbaseProfile(domain)
    ]);
    const siteInfo = exaData.status === 'fulfilled' ? exaData.value : null;
    ```

- Ensure partial results are returned even if some sources fail.

### Caching & Performance

- Cache frequent queries (in-memory or Redis) for a configurable TTL.
- Use Next.js ISR/SSG for static data where possible.
- Batch Exa queries to minimize API calls.

### Modularity & Type Safety

- Define TypeScript interfaces for all data entities.
- Abstract common logic (e.g., Exa search wrappers).
- Example:

    ```ts
    interface ExaResult { title: string; url: string; highlights: string[]; }
    async function findSimilarCompanies(url: string): Promise<ExaResult[]> {
      const exa = new Exa(process.env.EXA_API_KEY);
      return exa.findSimilarAndContents(url, { highlights: { numSentences: 2 }, numResults: 5 });
    }
    ```

- Organize code: `app/` (routes), `components/` (UI), `lib/` (services), `types/` (interfaces).

### API Routes vs Client Components

- Place all data-fetching in server-side route handlers.
- Only use client components for interactivity.
- Keep API keys and secrets on the server.

### Versioning & Extensibility

- Version API endpoints as needed (e.g., `/api/v1/company`).
- Write service layers to allow easy addition of new data sources.
- Follow SOLID principles for maintainability.

